This is a fork of https://github.com/ParBLiSS/parconnect, which removes most benchmarking code to provide a reusable library just containing the actual algorithm and its dependencies.

This also tries to fix some bugs and modernize the build process.

de Bruijn graph generator
-------------------------

`lib/graphGen/deBruijn/deBruijnGraphGen.hpp` is upstream's de Bruijn graph
generator: it builds the k=31 de Bruijn graph of one or more DNA read files with
BLISS and returns it as a distributed edge list. It is kept out of the
`parconnect` target because BLISS is a heavy dependency that nothing else needs
-- enable `PARCONNECT_ENABLE_DEBRUIJN` and link `parconnect::debruijn` instead.

`tools/dbg2parhip` wraps it into a converter, replacing upstream's
`utils_exportBinaryFormat` (which wrote one ad-hoc binary file per rank):

    mpirun -n <p> ./tools/dbg2parhip -i reads.fastq -o graph.parhip
    mpirun -n <p> ./tools/dbg2parhip -i reads_1.fastq reads_2.fastq -o graph.parhip

Input and output are named options, since with a variadic `-i` a positional
output would be ambiguous. Multiple input files are built into a single graph:
each is inserted into the same k-mer map and the map merges the edges of a k-mer
that is already present, so paired-end read files give exactly the graph of
their concatenation without concatenating them on disk.

Inputs need a `.fastq` extension -- BLISS dispatches its parser on the extension
and the generator fixes that parser to FASTQ, so `.fasta` is rejected -- and
must be uncompressed, with the DNA alphabet restricted to {A,C,G,T}. The output
is a single ParHiP graph, so any KaGen consumer can read it back with
`file;filename=graph.parhip;input_format=parhip`.

The tool compacts the packed-31-mer vertex ids to [0, n) (upstream's
`reduceVertexIds`), repartitions the edges so that each rank owns a contiguous
vertex block with all of its edges, and drops duplicate and self edges. The
result depends on neither the number of ranks nor the split of the reads across
input files: the output file is byte-identical for any `p`.

Both options default to ON for a top-level build and OFF when this project is
consumed via `FetchContent`, so a consumer of the connectivity algorithm alone
pulls in neither BLISS nor KaGen.
